INT. EMPTY GYM - SCOREBOARD CONTROL CLOSET - NIGHT

A cramped closet with a hand-wired switchboard. Taped wires. A dusty window onto the dark court below. A folding chair.

WILHELMINA "BILLIE" ROSSITER-KAMARA, 50s, former point guard, in a faded jersey and sweatpants, sits at the board with a clipboard on her lap. She taps a button. Below, the old scoreboard blinks: HOME 00 GUEST 00.

BILLIE
Check, check.

She taps another button. A horn blares through the empty gym. She winces, turns the volume down a notch.

BILLIE (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Twenty-two years and I still hit the wrong one.

A crackle on the intercom.

JANITOR (V.O.)
(over the PA)
Coach? You still in there?

BILLIE
(leaning to the mic)
Five more minutes, Eddie.

JANITOR (V.O.)
Lights go off at ten.

BILLIE
I know.

She hangs up the mic. Looks down through the window. The court is blank hardwood, the lines worn pale. She flips a switch. A spotlight lands on center court.

She sits for a while, watching the empty circle of light.

BILLIE (CONT'D)
(softly)
Tip off.

She presses the home-score button. HOME climbs: 02. She presses it again: 04. Then 06. Then 08. She presses it until it reads 99. A small, crooked smile.

She hits the horn one last time. It rolls across the gym, rattling the rafters.

BILLIE (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Good game.

She clicks off the spotlight. The scoreboard goes dark.
